  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 242 PM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nUpper low continues to rotate over northern MN and bring snow\nand breezy conditions through early evening, then pulling off to\nthe east later tonight. Northwest surface winds tonight across\nLake of the Woods could bring a bit more snow to our eastern\nborder even after the rest of the region tapers off. A bit of a\nbreak on Monday with a brief ridge between systems and high \npressure over most of the Plains, then another upper low comes \nin Tuesday and Wednesday. QPF signal from many of the models is \nfairly weak with the mid-week system and the NBM no longer has\nPOPs across our south like earlier runs. Can't completely rule\nout a trace of precipitation but looking less likely. The mid-\nweek system moves off into the Midwest Thursday, and then\nanother trough starts to move into the Prairie Provinces of\nCanada and then the Great Lakes Friday and Saturday. \n\n...Snow tapering off this evening...\n\nMoisture wrapping around the backside the low continues to bring\nsome snow and northwest winds around 30 mph to the northern Red\nRiver Valley and northwestern MN. A few spots get down to 1/4\nmile visibility for a time, mostly under the heavier falling\nsnow. Falling snow will taper off from west to east and the\npressure gradient will relax and winds come down later this\nevening as surface high pressure starts to build into the\nPlains.\n\n...Lake effect snow in far northeast tonight...\n\nNorthwest winds coming across Lake of the Woods, which is\ncurrently in the 40s F, will bring air temps in the 20s, even\ninto the teens. The large temperature difference will help drive\nsnow shower development even as the upper system exits off the\neast tonight. BUFKIT soundings from the HRRR and RAP have lake\ninduced CAPE over 200 J/kg later tonight. Exactly where lake\neffect snow will end up will depend on exact wind direction, but\ncould see another inch or two of snow around far northeastern\nportions of Lake of the Woods county. \n\n...Seasonable temps, then a bit of a warming trend...\n\nThe current upper low will help drag down fairly cold temps\ntonight into Monday, and readings will be on the colder side of\naverage for the first part of the upcoming week. However,\nheights start to come back up, and even with another trough\nmid-week the highs will be back near what is normal for late\nNovember. By Thursday and Friday, the ECMWF EFI is starting to\nshow a slight warm signal again, with temps back above average.",
